    {
      "id": 17,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        470,
        700
      ],
      "size": [
        470,
        300
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 16,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {
        "Node name for S&R": "Note"
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "REFMOD CONCEPT TEST \u2014 one boolean is the whole experiment\n\nWHAT THIS IS FOR\n  Not identity. A RefMod's DEFAULT extraction is a 4x4 latent = FOUR tokens,\n  and the upstream CLI defaults to `--mode training --pool 16` (64 tokens per\n  frame). Against our measured baseline \u2014 3 character anchors at\n  ref_image_size=max = 20,816 tokens, prompt 343 = 3.4% \u2014 a concept mod is\n  free and ADDITIVE. It does not displace the anchors: the bridge appends to\n  the same `minimax_refs` payload the reference node fills.\n\n  So this tests whether a cheap concept (body type, then pose) moves the\n  render, WITHOUT touching what the character references are doing.\n\nHOW TO RUN THE A/B\n  1. Fixed seed. It is already fixed; do not touch it between arms.\n  2. Render once with `enabled` OFF on H3 Simple RefMod Loader. That is the\n     control, and it is byte-identical to H3 02.\n  3. Flip `enabled` ON, render again. Same seed, same everything else.\n  4. Only then change one thing at a time: strength, then pool size, then the\n     dataset.\n\n  strength is NOT a LoRA weight. The tooltip is explicit: lower values blend\n  the reference toward a BLURRED latent. Start at 1.\n\nMAKING THE REFMOD \u2014 the part the GUI cannot do\n  H3 RefMod Studio (wherever you unpacked it) is ENCODE ONLY: full VAE\n  encode, zero refinement, 1024px / 8192 tokens. That is the identity path and\n  it is ~1024 tokens per image, 8 images to the cap.\n\n  The concept path needs the vendored CLI:\n\n    python extract_mod.py --mode training --pool 16 --concept-type pose_motion\n        --image a.png --image b.png ... --vae <video vae> --name my_concept\n\n  from <RefMod Studio>/h3_refmod_vendor, run with ComfyUI's own\n  Python. Output goes to ComfyUI/models/refmods.\n\nBUILD THE DATASET SO AVERAGING WORKS FOR YOU\n  The mechanism is adaptive_avg_pool3d \u2014 plain averaging. What survives is what\n  the images have in COMMON; everything else cancels. So a body-type or pose\n  concept wants MANY DIFFERENT SUBJECTS sharing only that attribute, with varied\n  faces, backgrounds and lighting. Built from one person, her face bleeds into\n  every render and fights the character anchors.\n\n  That is the opposite of identity-refmod advice (one person, many angles), and\n  it is why concepts suit this tool and identity does not.\n\nWHAT THE AUTHOR WARNS, AND IT IS IN THE CODE\n  - `concept_type` is descriptive METADATA. It isolates nothing; clothing, pose\n    and background stay in the latent whatever you label it.\n  - No semantic disentanglement: \"identity, clothing, background and composition\n    can still mix\".\n  - Fast motion smears.\n  - `--identity N` is MSE refinement steps against the full latent, not identity\n    learning. \"Increasing it does not teach the model which attribute to\n    preserve or discard.\"\n\nWHY THIS GRAPH AND NOT 38\n  Single chunk, short, one variable. The long-form recipe works now and a 4th\n  reference has already wrecked motion once \u2014 keep this away from it until a\n  concept mod is known-good here."
      ],
      "title": "How to use"
    },
